ilar to the sibling lectures (~30KB). I'll write carefully.\n\nFor the critical reading observation about w2/w3 swap — I'll present it in the weight module's 源码精读 as a \"批判性阅读\" callout, with the derivation. This is accurate and pedagogically aligned with the curriculum's goal of reading real imperfect code.\n\nLet me write the complete file now.\n\nOne more thing to verify for accuracy: the boundary check logic (lines 216-231). It checks:\n- if row < 12 AND any addr < 0 → set black (these are the top rows where dst_tl_y is negative, so backward-mapped source coords go negative/out of bounds)\n- else if row > 1080 AND any addr > 1100 → set black\n- else → interpolate\n\nThis handles out-of-bounds source addresses by zeroing. The magic numbers 12, 1080, 1100 are tied to the specific image (1100 rows). dst_tl.y = -550 (from .v: dst_tl_y = -550), so the top ~11 rows (550/50≈11) map to negative source coords. Actually the offset depends on scale. row index 0..11 correspond to dst_tl.y around -550..; the source coords map negative. So they zero them. This is a hardcoded boundary heuristic.\n\nActually, I shouldn't over-explain the boundary logic since it's not a core minimal module, but I'll mention it briefly as \"越界保护\" in the warp overview. Good.\n\nAlso note: at the end, warp calls OpenCV's own remap (line 262) and returns — so this whole addr/weight/res computation is the author's HAND-WRITTEN reimplementation of bilinear remap, running in parallel with OpenCV's remap, presumably to verify their hand-written version matches and to extract the addr.coe for FPGA. The `res.copyTo(dst)` is commented out (line 261), so the function actually returns OpenCV's remap result, while `res` (the hand-written one) is just saved to res.bmp for comparison. This is an important insight: the addr/weight/res block is a reference/verification implementation + coefficient extraction, not the actual pipeline output. I'll mention this clearly.\n\nNow writing.\n\nFor the addr memory layout: addr is CV_32SC1 (32-bit int, single channel), 1100 rows × 8688 cols. Each logical destination pixel occupies 8 consecutive int columns: (y0,x0,y1,x1,y2,x2,y3,x3). So 4 (row,col) pairs packed into one wide row. Similarly weight is CV_32FC1, 1100 × 4344, 4 floats per pixel.\n\nThis \"wide row packing\" (col*8, col*4) is a flat-memory way to store a per-pixel record. I'll explain this clearly as the \"内存组织方式\" which is a learning goal.\n\nWhy pack this way? Because it's meant to be dumped to .coe and loaded into FPGA Block RAM / distributed RAM as a lookup table: for destination pixel index `col`, you read 8 consecutive integers (the 4 source addresses) and 4 consecutive floats (the weights). It's a structure-of-arrays / record-per-pixel layout suitable for sequential BRAM reads. Good insight.\n\nLet me write now. I'll keep code snippets short and use permalinks.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the float weights and FPGA. The weight table holds floats (CV_32FC1). For FPGA, these would need to be quantized to fixed-point. The .v file instead computes weights on the fly from fractional parts (well, it computes the floor/ceil addresses; the actual fractional-weight multiply isn't shown). So the C++ weight table is a software reference; the hardware computes it. The addr.coe is the only thing exported. I'll explain this nuance.\n\nAlright, writing the file now.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2501,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":58176,"output_tokens":14211,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[],"speed":"standard"},"stop_details":null},"type":"assistant","uuid":"0165c56c-ecef-4dd2-a096-b116fd2ee2b6","timestamp":"2026-08-11T00:07:15.600Z","effort":"high","userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-cli","cwd":"/home/runner/work/code